1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
Section 4

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,799
This lecture will be about the science of acoustics, the study of sound, in

3
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:11,270
relation to urban environments such as cities.

4
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:16,799
As an acoustic engineer myself, I think this is an area where we're likely

5
00:00:16,799 --> 00:00:18,910
to see great changes.

6
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:23,830
In the past, researching urban soundscapes was simple.

7
00:00:23,830 --> 00:00:27,016
We measured levels of sound in decibels,

8
00:00:27,016 --> 00:00:32,427
so I used to take my sound meter and I measured the noise somewhere,

9
00:00:32,427 --> 00:00:36,915
and then I might ask a sample of people to say at what level

10
00:00:36,915 --> 00:00:39,240
the sound became annoying.

11
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:44,562
With data like this, acoustic engineers have been able to build up what we

12
00:00:44,562 --> 00:00:48,800
call noise maps, maps of the sound environment.

13
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,747
But actually these aren't a lot of use.

14
00:00:51,747 --> 00:00:59,069
What they do show is that the highest noise levels are generally on roads

15
00:00:59,069 --> 00:00:59,536
- well,

16
00:00:59,536 --> 00:01:01,870
that's not really very surprising.

17
00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:06,535
But there's quite a lot going on that these maps don't show,

18
00:01:06,535 --> 00:01:11,200
because they can't capture the complex way that sound varies over time.

19
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:17,635
So they ignore important issues such as the noise someone might hear from the open windows

20
00:01:17,635 --> 00:01:19,646
or gardens of their neighbours,

21
00:01:19,646 --> 00:01:24,070
and this sort of noise can be quite significant in summer.

22
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:28,022
We don't have any databases on this sort of information.

23
00:01:28,022 --> 00:01:33,555
As well as that, these records of sound levels take no account of the

24
00:01:33,555 --> 00:01:37,111
fact that people vary in their perceptions of noise -

25
00:01:37,111 --> 00:01:42,644
so someone like me with years of working in acoustics might be very different

26
00:01:42,644 --> 00:01:44,620
from you in that regard.

27
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:51,528
But anyway, even though these noise maps are fairly crude, they've been useful in providing information

28
00:01:51,528 --> 00:01:57,141
and raising awareness that noise matters, we need to deal with it and

29
00:01:57,141 --> 00:01:59,300
so it's a political matter.

30
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:05,954
And that's important - we need rules and regulations because noise can cause all

31
00:02:05,954 --> 00:02:07,490
sorts of problems.

32
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:12,650
Those of you who are city-dwellers know that things go on 24 hours a

33
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:13,019
day,

34
00:02:13,019 --> 00:02:15,599
so city-dwellers often suffer from interrupted sleep.

35
00:02:15,599 --> 00:02:20,759
It's also known that noise can lead to a rise in levels of stress,

36
00:02:20,759 --> 00:02:25,550
due to physical changes in the body affecting the composition of the blood.

37
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:31,086
And there are other problems as well, for instance if schoolchildren don't have a

38
00:02:31,086 --> 00:02:34,250
quiet place to study their work will suffer.

39
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:40,576
Now, one problem with decibel measurement is that it doesn't differentiate between different types

40
00:02:40,576 --> 00:02:41,480
of noise.

41
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:46,325
Some types of sounds that most people would probably think of as nice

42
00:02:46,325 --> 00:02:51,170
and relaxing might well score quite highly in decibel levels - think of the

43
00:02:51,170 --> 00:02:55,270
sound made by a fountain in a town square, for example.

44
00:02:55,270 --> 00:03:00,140
That's not necessarily something that we'd want to control or reduce.

45
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:05,660
So maybe researchers should consider these sorts of sounds in urban design.

46
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:11,005
This is going to be tricky because just measuring decibel levels isn't going to

47
00:03:11,005 --> 00:03:12,150
help us here.

48
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:20,922
Instead, many researchers are using social science techniques, studying people's emotional response to sound by using questionnaires

49
00:03:20,922 --> 00:03:22,470
and so on.

50
00:03:22,470 --> 00:03:27,039
So what exactly do people want to hear in an urban environment?

51
00:03:27,039 --> 00:03:32,370
Some recent interdisciplinary research has come out with results that at first sight seem

52
00:03:32,370 --> 00:03:35,797
contradictory city needs to have a sense of activity,

53
00:03:35,797 --> 00:03:42,271
so it needs to be lively, with sounds like the clack of high heels on a pavement

54
00:03:42,271 --> 00:03:44,936
or the hiss of a coffee machine,

55
00:03:44,936 --> 00:03:50,267
but these mustn't be too intrusive because at the same time we need to

56
00:03:50,267 --> 00:03:51,790
be able to relax.

57
00:03:51,790 --> 00:03:56,268
One of the major problems in achieving this will be getting architects

58
00:03:56,268 --> 00:03:58,880
and town planners to use the research.

59
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:04,821
Apart from studying the basics of acoustics, these people receive very little training in

60
00:04:04,821 --> 00:04:05,670
this area.

61
00:04:05,670 --> 00:04:10,526
But in fact they should be regarding sound as an opportunity to add to

62
00:04:10,526 --> 00:04:16,770
the experience of urban living, whereas at present they tend to see it as something to be avoided

63
00:04:16,770 --> 00:04:18,851
or reduced as far as possible,

64
00:04:18,851 --> 00:04:23,360
or something that's just a job for engineers like the street drainage system.

65
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:28,522
What's needed is for noise in cities to be regarded as an aesthetic quality,

66
00:04:28,522 --> 00:04:32,210
as something that has the qualities of an art form.

67
00:04:32,210 --> 00:04:37,085
If we acknowledge this, then we urgently need to know what governs it

68
00:04:37,085 --> 00:04:39,710
and how designers can work with it.

69
00:04:39,710 --> 00:04:43,460
We need to develop a complex understanding of many factors.

70
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:47,790
What is the relationship between sound and culture?

71
00:04:47,790 --> 00:04:55,187
What can we learn from disciplines such as psychology about the way that sound interacts with human development

72
00:04:55,187 --> 00:04:56,420
and social relationships,

73
00:04:56,420 --> 00:05:00,530
and the way that sound affects our thought and feelings?

74
00:05:00,530 --> 00:05:05,580
Can we learn anything from physics about the nature of sound itself?

75
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:08,668
Today's powerful technologies can also help us.

76
00:05:08,668 --> 00:05:16,167
To show us their ideas and help us to imagine the effect their buildings will have, architects

77
00:05:16,167 --> 00:05:19,254
and town planners already use virtual reality,

78
00:05:19,254 --> 00:05:21,460
but these programs are silent.

79
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:28,211
In the future such programs could use realistic sounds, meaning that soundscapes could be

80
00:05:28,211 --> 00:05:30,140
explored before being built.

81
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:35,402
So hopefully, using the best technology we can lay our hands on, the city

82
00:05:35,402 --> 00:05:40,664
of the future will be a pleasure to the ears as well as the

83
00:05:40,664 --> 00:05:41,040
eyes.
